  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study identifies task-associated neurons and applies inference-time interventions to steer behavior, reporting steering performance. It does not test whether neuron poles have a named main job or validate neuron explanations on held-out activations.",
    "gate_note": "The reported outcomes concern steering between memorization and generalization on synthetic tasks, not the neuron interpretability, explanation accuracy, or attribution-graph claims in V1–V7. The steering outcomes are recorded as findings about neuron-based control, with versions [\"none\"]. Values cited from other work are not included.",
    "test_reason": "The study selects memorization- and generalization-associated neurons by correlation and mean activation difference, then reports how well its own steering works. It never names neuron jobs, audits shares of interpretable neurons, scores explanations or builds attribution graphs. So it bears on none of V1–V7. T6 would also be defensible: it neither cites nor relies on the essay's claim beyond assuming that neuron-level steering is meaningful.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Accurate. The authors conclude that memorization and generalization activate distinct neuron subsets, concentrated in deeper layers. Intervening on these neurons steers behaviour at inference time, and the associations persist across retrained adapters and partly transfer across tasks. They read this as behaviours 'encoded in separable neural pathways', and note limits in model, task and intervention scope.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses task-specific neuron correlations and activation shifts to steer memorization/generalization; it does not generate natural-language neuron explanations or test them against held-out activations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Transfer effectiveness differs by task direction: arithmetic-addition neurons transfer more effectively to in-context inference than in-context-inference neurons transfer to arithmetic addition (Table 4).",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Shifting the top-correlated neurons by their mean activation difference steers outputs between the memorized and generalized answer on two synthetic tasks. Success is higher Mem→Gen (e.g. GPT-2 ICL 83.7%) than Gen→Mem (35.8%). Steering partly carries over to retrained adapters and transfers asymmetrically across tasks. Random neuron shifts yield 100% 'Other'.",
        "proposed_claim": "Small sets of behaviour-correlated neurons can steer memorization versus generalization in synthetic tasks, with effectiveness depending on direction and task.",
