    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing",
    "problems": "The evidence is limited to two synthetic task types and GPT-2/GPT-2-medium and LLaMA 3.2-3B; the authors note broader model and task validation is needed. Generalization is operationalized in limited task-specific ways. The reported steering results do not measure whether neurons have human-nameable main jobs or whether explanations predict activations on held-out inputs; uncertainty estimates are not reported for the tabulated outcomes.",
    "rows_left_out": "none",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No audit of the share of Qwen3-32B (or any model's) neuron poles with one main job, two jobs, or not understood."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No interpretability shares across models, scales, dense or MoE architectures. Only GPT-2, GPT-2-medium and LLaMA 3.2-3B, used for steering."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No vision models, no InceptionV1 cat/car neuron, no analysis of polysemanticity versus superposition."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No comparison of architectures, regularization or explanation tools on the interpretable-neuron share."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "No attribution graphs and no comparison with replacement models or reconstruction error."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "No natural-language neuron explanations tested against held-out activations."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No simulation-based scoring of neuron explanations."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Rows are the study's own steering-method results, correctly linked to 'none'. For r1–r20, baseline 'not stated' is effectively 0% target behaviour: interventions are applied to cases already showing the opposite behaviour. The supplement's pairwise-dataset list appears to swap the model labels (GPT2 for in-context, GPT2-medium for arithmetic), contradicting the training notes and the Figure 2 caption. The rows follow the training notes, which seems right. In r16 the Gen→Mem transfer largely fails: 63.2% of outputs stay Gen and only 22.8% shift to Mem."
  },
